# The CRLF/NUL guard (the world repo's 304890d2 lesson, office copy — Keemin yes 2026-08-23):
# a stray CR or NUL in a source file must not flip git into binary-sniffing a
# .mjs/.md diff into a Bin sweep. Text, LF, always.
*.mjs text eol=lf
*.md text eol=lf
# POS-60: .sh and .yml join the guard for a second reason on top of that one —
# these two run on Linux. A shell script committed with CRLF from a Windows
# checkout fails on the box with `bash: \r: command not found`, and the deploy
# script here IS the deploy. Text, LF, always.
*.sh text eol=lf
*.yml text eol=lf
*.yaml text eol=lf

# Systemd units run on the box. A CRLF in ExecStart fails as a missing file.
*.service text eol=lf
*.timer text eol=lf
# …and so do their DROP-INS, which the two lines above did not cover (found
# 2026-09-10 while adding deploy/postmark-world2-office-tree.conf). A drop-in is
# read by systemd exactly like the unit it extends: a CRLF here makes
# `Environment=WORLD2_OFFICE=/srv/postmark-office` set a path with a trailing CR,
# and every lane that `cd`s into it fails looking like a missing directory. The
# nginx confs on the box are the same argument from the other side.
*.conf text eol=lf

# G1 lane 3b: the photograph fixtures are BYTE-EXACT copies of files the town
# committed into the world repo — `test/fixtures/177.journal.jsonl` is world main
# `256db2fe:STATE/log/177.journal.jsonl`, sha256 b9ab7268…. `core.autocrlf=true`
# on a Windows checkout would rewrite those bytes, and the byte-equality
# falsifier would then be comparing the register against a file the town never
# wrote — the same guard as above, for the same reason, on the one extension
# that carries evidence rather than source.
test/fixtures/** text eol=lf
